{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The senior ABiH officer exercised effective command and control over the troops that perpetrated the murders in Grabovica, satisfying the effective authority element of command responsibility under Article 7(3) of the ICTY Statute.",
      "reasoning": "The officer held the position of Chief of the Main Staff and was appointed leader of an Inspection Team with coordinating and monitoring functions over the 4th and 6th Corps, which included the 9th Motorised Brigade that carried out the killings. His authority to issue orders \"in keeping with his authority\" and to consult the Commander on drastic proposals demonstrates functional control over the forces, meeting the \"effective command and control\" requirement articulated in the Rome Statute and reflected in customary law.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "customary",
          "instrument": "customary_ihl",
          "rule_number": 152,
          "quoted_text": "Commanders and other superiors are criminally responsible for war crimes committed pursuant to their orders."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "28",
          "quoted_text": "A military commander or person effectively acting as a military commander shall be criminally responsible for crimes within the jurisdiction of the Court committed by forces under his or her effective command and control, or effective authority and control as the case may be, as a result of his or her failure to exercise control properly over such forces, where: (i) That military commander or person either knew or, owing to the circumstances at the time, should have known that the forces were committing or about to commit such crimes; and (ii) That military commander or person failed to take all necessary and reasonable measures within his or her power to prevent or repress their commission or to submit the matter to the competent authorities for investigation and prosecution."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The officer knew, or at the very least should have known, that the 9th Brigade was committing murders of civilians on 8–9 September 1993.",
      "reasoning": "Given his coordinating role, his presence in the area, and the fact that troops were billeted with the civilian population, the officer had constructive knowledge of the operations. He learned of the killings on the evening of 9 September, which is contemporaneous with the crimes, indicating that the information was readily available to him and that a reasonable commander in his position would have been aware of the unlawful conduct as it unfolded.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "customary",
          "instrument": "customary_ihl",
          "rule_number": 153,
          "quoted_text": "Commanders and other superiors are criminally responsible for war crimes committed by their subordinates if they knew, or had reason to know, that the subordinates were about to commit or were committing such crimes and did not take all necessary and reasonable measures in their power to prevent their commission, or if such crimes had been committed, to punish the persons responsible."
